Usage: wamrc [options] -o output_file wasm_file
  --target=<arch-name>      Set the target arch, which has the general format: <arch><sub>
                            <arch> = x86_64, i386, aarch64, arm, thumb, xtensa, mips,
                                     riscv64, riscv32.
                              Default is host arch, e.g. x86_64
                            <sub> = for ex. on arm or thumb: v5, v6m, v7a, v7m, etc.
                            Use --target=help to list supported targets
                            Or, provide a triple in the format of <arch>-<vendor>-<os>-<abi>.
                            By doing this, --target-abi, --cpu, and --cpu-features will be ignored.
                            The triple will only be normalized without any further verification.
  --target-abi=<abi>        Set the target ABI, e.g. gnu, eabi, gnueabihf, msvc, etc.
                              Default is gnu if target isn't riscv64 or riscv32
                              For target riscv64 and riscv32, default is lp64d and ilp32d
                            Use --target-abi=help to list all the ABI supported
  --cpu=<cpu>               Set the target CPU (default: host CPU, e.g. skylake)
                            Use --cpu=help to list all the CPU supported
  --cpu-features=<features> Enable or disable the CPU features
                            Use +feature to enable a feature, or -feature to disable it
                            For example, --cpu-features=+feature1,-feature2
                            Use --cpu-features=+help to list all the features supported
  --opt-level=n             Set the optimization level (0 to 3, default is 3)
  --size-level=n            Set the code size level (0 to 3, default is 3)
                              0 - Large code model
                              1 - Medium code model
                              2 - Kernel code model
                              3 - Small code model
  -sgx                      Generate code for SGX platform (Intel Software Guard Extensions)
  --bounds-checks=1/0       Enable or disable the bounds checks for memory access:
                              This flag controls bounds checking with a software check. 
                              On 64-bit platforms, it is disabled by default, using a hardware 
                              trap if supported, except when SGX or memory64 is enabled,
                              which defaults to a software check.
                              On 32-bit platforms, the flag is enabled by default, using a software check
                              due to the lack of hardware support.
                            CAVEAT: --bounds-checks=0 enables some optimizations
                              which make the compiled AOT module incompatible
                              with a runtime without the hardware bounds checks.
  --stack-bounds-checks=1/0 Enable or disable the bounds checks for native stack:
                              if the option isn't set, the status is same as `--bounds-check`,
                              if the option is set, the status is same as the option value
  --stack-usage=<file>      Generate a stack-usage file.
                              Similarly to `clang -fstack-usage`.
  --format=<format>         Specifies the format of the output file
                            The format supported:
                              aot (default)  AoT file
                              object         Native object file
                              llvmir-unopt   Unoptimized LLVM IR
                              llvmir-opt     Optimized LLVM IR
  --disable-bulk-memory     Disable the MVP bulk memory feature
  --enable-multi-thread     Enable multi-thread feature, the dependent features bulk-memory and
                            thread-mgr will be enabled automatically
  --enable-tail-call        Enable the post-MVP tail call feature
  --disable-simd            Disable the post-MVP 128-bit SIMD feature:
                              currently 128-bit SIMD is supported for x86-64 and aarch64 targets,
                              and by default it is enabled in them and disabled in other targets
  --disable-ref-types       Disable the MVP reference types feature, it will be disabled forcibly if
                              GC is enabled
  --disable-aux-stack-check Disable auxiliary stack overflow/underflow check
  --enable-dump-call-stack  Enable stack trace feature
  --call-stack-features=<features>
                            A comma-separated list of features when generating call stacks.
                            By default, all features are enabled. To disable all features,
                            provide an empty list (i.e. --call-stack-features=). This flag
                            only only takes effect when --enable-dump-call-stack is set.
                            Available features: bounds-checks, ip, func-idx, trap-ip, values.
  --enable-perf-profiling   Enable function performance profiling
  --enable-memory-profiling Enable memory usage profiling
  --xip                     A shorthand of --enable-indirect-mode --disable-llvm-intrinsics
  --enable-indirect-mode    Enable call function through symbol table but not direct call
  --enable-gc               Enable GC (Garbage Collection) feature
  --disable-llvm-intrinsics Disable the LLVM built-in intrinsics
  --enable-builtin-intrinsics=<flags>
                            Enable the specified built-in intrinsics, it will override the default
                              settings. It only takes effect when --disable-llvm-intrinsics is set.
                            Available flags: all, i32.common, i64.common, f32.common, f64.common,
                              i32.clz, i32.ctz, etc, refer to doc/xip.md for full list
                            Use comma to separate, please refer to doc/xip.md for full list.
  --disable-llvm-jump-tables Disable the LLVM jump tables similarly to clang's -fno-jump-tables
  --disable-llvm-lto        Disable the LLVM link time optimization
  --enable-llvm-pgo         Enable LLVM PGO (Profile-Guided Optimization)
  --enable-llvm-passes=<passes>
                            Enable the specified LLVM passes, using comma to separate
  --use-prof-file=<file>    Use profile file collected by LLVM PGO (Profile-Guided Optimization)
  --enable-segue[=<flags>]  Enable using segment register GS as the base address of linear memory,
                            only available on linux x86-64, which may improve performance,
                            flags can be: i32.load, i64.load, f32.load, f64.load, v128.load,
                                          i32.store, i64.store, f32.store, f64.store, v128.store
                            Use comma to separate, e.g. --enable-segue=i32.load,i64.store
                            and --enable-segue means all flags are added.
  --emit-custom-sections=<section names>
                            Emit the specified custom sections to AoT file, using comma to separate
                            multiple names, e.g.
                                --emit-custom-sections=section1,section2,sectionN
  --native-lib=<lib>        Register native libraries to the WASM module, which
                            are shared object (.so) files, for example:
                              --native-lib=test1.so --native-lib=test2.so
  --invoke-c-api-import     Treat unknown import function as wasm-c-api import function and
                            quick call it from AOT code
  --enable-linux-perf       Enable linux perf support
  --mllvm=<option>          Add the LLVM command line option
  --enable-shared-heap      Enable shared heap feature, assuming only one shared heap will be attached
  --enable-shared-chain     Enable shared heap chain feature, works for more than one shared heap
                            WARNING: enable this feature will largely increase code size
  -v=n                      Set log verbose level (0 to 5, default is 2), larger with more log
  --version                 Show version information
Examples: wamrc -o test.aot test.wasm
          wamrc --target=i386 -o test.aot test.wasm
          wamrc --target=i386 --format=object -o test.o test.wasm
          wamrc --target-abi=help
          wamrc --target=x86_64 --cpu=help
